(* --- Paragraphs --- *)
(* A VISIBLE block (heading, quote, table, fence, thematic break,
   admonition/div) interrupts a paragraph with no blank line before it
   (Markdown-like); invisible constructs (reference definitions, comments,
   block-attribute lines) interrupt too. A LIST marker (bullet OR ordered) is
   the exception: it does NOT interrupt -- a list needs a blank line before it
   (symmetric, Djot-like; see §10). This is the semantic constraint
   PART 9 §10, not a context-free one. A paragraph is terminated by a blank
   line, an interrupting block (§10), or end of file -- the optional trailing
   blank_line here is the paragraph's own terminator, not a precondition for
   the NEXT block.
   NO TRAILING WHITESPACE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-025]. A `whitespace` run at the END of a
   CONTENT LINE is DROPPED. It does not reach the output, and it is not
   content.

   The rule holds for every content line.
   `abc<SP>` renders <p>abc</p>; so does `abc<SP>` followed by `def`, where the
   run sits before a SOFT BREAK; and so do a heading, a list item, a block
   quote line, a definition term or description, a footnote body line, a table
   caption, a line-block line and a `%%` COMMENT LINE's text -- where exactly
   ONE space or tab after the marker is the separator and is consumed, so
   `%%<SP><SP>x<SP>` has the text `<SP>x`.

   THE RUN IS `whitespace` -- `' ' | '\t'` -- the same two-character terminal
   `blank_line = {whitespace}` and `indent` take (PART 1). Every
   other character is CONTENT and survives, however invisible: a no-break
   space, a zero-width space, a BYTE ORDER MARK, an en quad, an ideographic
   space, a form feed and a vertical tab all reach the output. In
   `<SP>U+FEFF<SP>` the BOM is content; only the trailing SPACE is dropped.

   WHERE IT DOES NOT REACH. Verbatim content keeps its bytes: a fenced code
   block's body, a raw block's body and a `%%%` COMMENT BLOCK's body are not
   content LINES in this sense, they are the block's payload. Whitespace INSIDE
   a construct is not trailing either -- `x<SP>` inside a code span, a literal
   inline or a table cell ends at the construct's delimiter, not at the line's
   end, and the line-break backslash likewise leaves the run before it
   interior.

   THE LINE BLOCK IS NOT AN EXCEPTION: PART 9 §23 MEDIAL GAPS converts a
   INNER or TRAILING run of TWO OR MORE columns into N\P content before this rule applies.
   Its one-column case remains collapsible whitespace and is dropped at EOL.

   (Carve has no two-trailing-space hard break -- a hard break is the backslash
   form, `\` + newline; see PART 3. This rule is why: a document cannot carry
   meaning in whitespace nothing can see.) Pinned by corpus
   104-paragraph-trailing-whitespace, 268 and 498. *)
paragraph = inline_content+, [blank_line] ;

